--- What the builder actually fetches ---

build_srd.py reads exactly two upstreams, and the sections below say what it
takes from each. Both are fetched over HTTPS at build time and neither is
vendored into this repository, so the commit shas in the generated dataset are
what tie a copy of the data back to these sources.

From 5e-bits/5e-srd-api, branch `main`, at
`packages/5e-database/src/2014/en/` (the English 2014 ruleset, which is where
those records now live; the standalone 5e-bits/5e-database repository is
archived and no longer updated):

    5e-SRD-Spells.json       spells
    5e-SRD-Equipment.json    equipment
    5e-SRD-Magic-Items.json  magic_items
    5e-SRD-Conditions.json   conditions
    5e-SRD-Monsters.json     monsters

Spells, equipment, magic items, conditions and monsters in
data/dnd5e_srd.json come from there. That package's own LICENSE.md is MIT
(Copyright (c) [2018-2020] [Adrian Padua, Christopher Ward]). MIT covers the
packaging, not the SRD text inside it; the grant relied on for the text is
CC-BY-4.0 above.

From foundryvtt/dnd5e, branch `master`, under `packs/_source`: the `classes`
pack (the twelve core classes), `classfeatures` and `races`. Those YAML
documents are parsed with PyYAML. That repository's own LICENSE is MIT
(Copyright 2021 Andrew Clayton); it likewise covers the packaging, and the
SRD-derived feature text inside is under the CC-BY-4.0 grant above. Only that
content is used, and none of that repository's other content or assets are
bundled here.

This project targets the 2014 rules and does not read Foundry's 2024 packs
(`classes24`, `spells24`, `origins24`, `feats24`), which carry SRD 5.2 material
under the same CC-BY-4.0 terms (SRD 5.2, https://www.dndbeyond.com/srd).

`build_srd.py --no-fvtt` skips the foundryvtt half entirely. That dataset holds
spells, equipment, magic items, conditions and monsters only, with an empty
`features` list, so none of the Foundry-derived text above is in it.

The race list follows the 2014 books and is wider than the SRD 5.1 subset
included in the section above, so a redistributor should confirm their own
obligations for that text rather than relying on the SRD 5.1 grant to cover it.

--- What the generated file says about itself ---

data/dnd5e_srd.json is generated by build_srd.py and is gitignored, so a clean
checkout does not have one. Its `_meta` block carries the pointers that a
consumer of the file alone needs, which is the case this section exists for: a
redistributed dataset is no longer in this repository and cannot fall back on
this file.

    edition        Which ruleset the records are. This build writes "2014".

    built_at       UTC timestamp of the build that produced the file.

    total_records  Every record in the file, all categories together.

    record_counts  Records per category, which agrees with the arrays.

    license        The grant the CONTENT is used under: the CC-BY-4.0
                   identifier, its name and url, what it covers, and the path
                   to this file.

    attribution    The credit CC-BY-4.0 requires (the SRD 5.1 credit), the
                   modifications made, and the path to this file.

    sources        Per upstream: repo, path, branch, sha and fetched_at, plus
                   that repository's own `license` and `license_url`.

The per-source licenses are both MIT and neither is the content license. They
are recorded separately on purpose: a reader shown only the MIT one would take
away that the SRD text carries no attribution duty, which is the specific
mistake this block is here to prevent.

A source `sha` is recorded as the empty string when its tree could not be read,
rather than guessed at, because a wrong sha is worse than a missing one. An
empty sha there means "provenance unavailable", not "no license".

